## 3. ERROR HANDLING

### ❌ BEFORE (No Error Handling)
```javascript
app.get('/users/:id', (req, res) => {
  res.json(users[req.params.id]); 
  // If ID is invalid, crashes or returns undefined
  // No error handling, stack trace exposed
});

// Unhandled error in production → server crash or data leak
```

### ✅ AFTER (Complete Error Handling)
```javascript
// Error handling middleware
const errorHandler = (err, req, res, next) => {
  const isDev = process.env.NODE_ENV !== 'production';

  let status = err.status || 500;
  let message = err.message || 'Internal Server Error';

  if (err.message && err.message.includes('must be')) {
    status = 400;
  }

  const errorResponse = {
    status: 'error',
    message: isDev ? message : 'An error occurred',
    ...(isDev && { stack: err.stack }), // Only in dev
  };

  console.error(`[${new Date().toISOString()}] Error:`, err);
  res.status(status).json(errorResponse);
};

// Route with error handling
app.get('/users/:id', (req, res, next) => {
  try {
    const id = validatePositiveInteger(req.params.id, 'User ID');
    const user = mockUsers[id];

    if (!user) {
      const err = new Error('User not found');
      err.status = 404;
      throw err;
    }

    res.status(200).json({
      status: 'success',
      data: { user }
    });
  } catch (err) {
    next(err); // Pass to error middleware
  }
});

// Apply error middleware LAST
app.use(errorHandler);
```

**Improvements**:
- ✅ All errors caught with try-catch
- ✅ Proper HTTP status codes
- ✅ No stack trace leakage in production
- ✅ Structured error responses
- ✅ Server logging for debugging

